@ianswett points out that the obvious solution to #3765 is for the node to respond to the attempted probe with a stateless reset.  Clients would see a Stateless Reset while attempting to probe and not use the new path.  That was, in fact, part of the original design IIRC.

However, the reason we removed that was that doing so opens up an attack.  An attacker can take a packet, rewrite the source address to an IP range the server always rejects, and get back a valid SRT for that CID.  It can then inject the SRT toward the client to destroy the connection at any time, albeit only until the CID is rotated out.

As far as I can tell, this attack isn't documented anywhere.  The right fix is to note that if servers receive packets that would be associated with an existing connection but would not permit migration, they SHOULD drop the packet and MUST NOT generate a stateless reset.

