@rpaulo:
> Negotiating extensions as part of the handshake is important, but do we need the {0,0xffff} -> {0x80, 0xff} mapping? Are we expecting this mapping to be maintained for multiple QUIC versions?

I think that we're building much of QUICv1 with the expectation that QUICv2+ won't change it.  I think of it more from the perspective of future simplicity: we wouldn't want to rebuild all QUICv1 extensions and applications just because we added (for example) multipath in a later version.

@martinthomson Thank you for pointing out that it is necessary to use TP for negotiating some features, and also for moving the negotiation there.

@ekr 
> Well, as you acknowledge below, it is feature negotiation for features that need new frames.

I share this model, and see two issues with the proposed approach:
* The proposed approach has two ways of extending QUIC, one by assigning a new TransportParameterId and one by assigning one or more `extension_frame_identifier`. I think that we should better unify the two schemes.
* We might want to pass along some parameters for the extension being negotiated. However, the proposal does not allow an `ExtensionFrameMappings` to carry such parameters.

Considering the two, how about defining the frame types to be used for each extension as a direct member of `TransportParameter`, like below?

```
struct {
  TransportParameterId parameter;
  opaque value<0..2^16-1>;
  uint8 frame_types<0..128>;        // <- this!
} TransportParameter;
```

Then, we can simplify the procedure of adding new extensions, regardless of if it needs frame types or not. An extension would always have a new TransportParameterId, can have any argument passed by using `value`, and have any number of frame types being assigned. We will only need one IANA registry for TransportParameterIds.

@kazuho As far as I can tell any PN scheme where you try to use the encrypted PN as IV cannot be both secret and short because it must be a permutation and a secure encryption would require repeating values based on a longer key. Anything shorter can be trivially brute forced. The question is if that bruteforce is sufficient since middleware won't have time to do it online.

As to the PN verification I believe you either have to check the entire AEAD packet, or add a short checksum. I suggest a short checksum because it can reject DoS attacks quickly and without access to the full traffic key.

In any case, privacy is not contained in any short AEAD independent scheme as it is too easy to crack, at least if you want encrypted PN as IV. Therefore the separate discussion of privacy without encryption / shuffle.

@kazuho

> As far as I understand, any PNE scheme without accompanying checksum is fragile to side-channel attacks, because the probability of the receiver dropping the packet without decrypting the payload is proportional to the value of the decrypted PN field. An attacker that has access to side-channel information can use that information to guess the value of the PN field.

The PR mandates that receivers not do that (though I concede that that could easily turn into a 6919 "MUST (BUT WE KNOW YOU WON'T)").  In short, the checksum you are looking for is the integrity check that is integrated into the AEAD.

